      "id": 1,
      "text": "Middle River earned its name as the midpoint along the 19th‑century Pembina Trail that linked Pembina to Fort Snelling.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/middle_river_minnesota",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town is known as Minnesota’s Goose Capital, celebrated for its prime goose hunting and proximity to major wildlife habitats.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/middle_river_minnesota",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Every year, residents gather for Goose Fest, the last weekend of September, drawing hunters and nature lovers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/middle_river_minnesota",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Ancient Lake Agassiz beach ridges and sediments shape the valley, with elevations dropping from about 1,200 to 800 feet over 17 miles.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/middle_river_minnesota",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "USGS records at Argyle show the Middle River’s flow swing from a dry 1.6 cfs in 1977 to over 180 cfs in wet 2022.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/middle_river_minnesota",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The river crosses an old Lake Agassiz beach ridge at Old Mill State Park, flowing southwesterly through a valley carved by the ancient lake.",
